{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"$\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]$ is the *second derivative* of $\\ln(2x+4)$. In other words, it's the derivative of $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]$, which is the derivative of $\\ln(2x+4)$.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]$, we need to differentiate $\\ln(2x+4)$ twice.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"Let's start by finding $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]$:\n\n$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]=\\dfrac{1}{x+2}$\n\n[[☃ explanation 1]]","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{"explanation 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"explanation":"$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2x+4}\\right)\\cdot\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[(2x+4)\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2x+4}\\right)\\cdot2\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{2}{2x+4}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{1}{x+2}\n\\end{align}$","hidePrompt":"Got it, thanks!","showPrompt":"Show entire calculation.","static":false,"widgets":{}},"static":false,"type":"explanation","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}},{"content":"Now we can differentiate $\\dfrac{1}{x+2}$ to find $\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]$:\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{x+2}\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x+2}\\right)^2\n\\end{align}$\n\n[[☃ explanation 1]]","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{"explanation 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"explanation":"$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{x+2}\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[(x+2)^{-1}\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-(x+2)^{-2}\\cdot\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[x+2\\right]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-(x+2)^{-2}\\cdot1\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x+2}\\right)^2\\end{align}$","hidePrompt":"Got it, thanks!","showPrompt":"Show entire calculation.","static":false,"widgets":{}},"static":false,"type":"explanation","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}},{"content":"In conclusion, $\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x+2}\\right)^2$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Find $\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}\\left[\\ln(2x+4)\\right]$.**\n\n[[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"considered":"correct","form":false,"key":0,"simplify":false,"value":"-\\left(\\frac{1}{x+2}\\right)^2"}],"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
